  "rationale": "**Holds for native and canonical outputs.** `handle_session_start` joins built-in fragments; `execute_hook` passes the built-in context as `prior_context`; `JoinedContext::new` starts from it and `JoinedContext::add` (called after each successful hook in `dispatch_plugin_hooks`) appends each hook's `additional_context()` with a blank line, only on `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit` and `PostToolUse`, and writes the joined text back over the context the hook's merge just replaced. Tests (pass): `tests/hook_context.rs` `session_start_keeps_symposium_and_plugin_context` (built-in consent notice, then `first`, then `second`, in order, for Claude, Codex, Copilot, Kiro, Antigravity) and `post_tool_use_keeps_every_plugin_context` (Claude, Codex, Copilot, Kiro). `cargo test -q --test hook_context`: 2 passed. `src/hook.rs` `symposium_output_serializes_with_additional_context` only checks `OutputEvent::with_context` (weak).\n\n**Why false.** A symposium-format hook whose output is a JSON object but not an `OutputEvent` (the `raw_json_fallback` branch) gets `hook_output = None`, so its context is never added to the joined text. Its raw context field is merged, then overwritten by the next `add` re-merge whenever joined text exists. That source supplied context and the response doesn't carry it. Either fix the fallback or narrow this text to native and canonical outputs (chapter note on `raw_json_fallback` records this gap).",
  "requirement": "On `SessionStart`, `UserPromptSubmit` and `PostToolUse`, the response MUST carry the context of every source that supplied one, the built-in context first and then each plugin hook's in dispatch order."
}
